From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Quarrier <squarrier@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 20:37:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] glm5_next/cuda: prompt chunks fill as pairs while streams
 decode, TF_GLM_FILL_PAIRS and TF_GLM_FILL_ROUNDS (extends MiaAI-Lab recipe
 patch 0062's sliced fills)

With TF_GLM_FILL_BUDGET_MS (MiaAI-Lab recipe patch 0062, ported) a prompt chunk started while other streams decode
runs a few layers at a time, one decode round between two slices. On the attention/expert split such a chunk runs
alone: the 5090 or the two Sparks idle while the other works, one exchange on the wire at a time. In the release
configuration's arena cells a 2,105-row fill at 64K of context runs ~2,500 tok/s as a pair (TF_GLM_PREFILL_PAIRS,
ours) and ~1,300 tok/s in slices, and a decode round with a slice due takes ~250 ms against ~75 ms without one: at
10 requests x 64K each reply decodes at 12.0 tok/s, mostly because its rounds wait behind those slices.

- TF_GLM_FILL_PAIRS=1 (fill_pairs.py): on the AFD attention node, while streams decode, a new prompt chunk and the
  next one fill as one unsliced pair (MultiDecoder._pair_plan's rules, busy=True: the first chunk ends no prompt, no
  stream drafts with the MTP head, at most two chunks of TF_GLM_FILL_ROWS rows), then the decode rounds due after a
  slice; a chunk without a partner goes in slices as before. Acts with TF_GLM_PREFILL_PAIRS=1 (or LANES >= 2) and
  TF_GLM_FILL_BUDGET_MS > 0 on the attention node, and does nothing elsewhere (as TF_GLM_PREFILL_PAIRS off the
  node). /health: "fill_pairs" {pairs, rows}.
- TF_GLM_FILL_ROUNDS=K (1 .. 8, default 1): the decode rounds due after each slice or such pair before the next fill
  step (MultiDecoder.decode_due is that count; 1 is the ported behaviour), with TF_GLM_FILL_BUDGET_MS > 0, at any
  world (rank 1 applies rank 0's ROUND and FILLPART messages as they come).

Neither changes a reply: each chunk keeps its rows, row count and kernels (a pair only interleaves two chunks'
kernels), and each decode round's streams get their solo tokens whichever forwards come between. Only the order of
whole forwards changes; the expert nodes serve MODE_PAIR and decode forwards as they come (wire unchanged). With both
unset the decoder, its messages, /health and the start line are as before. A timing model of the release
configuration's 10 x 64K cell (fitted to two boots' arena cells): TF_GLM_FILL_PAIRS=1 per-request decode +28 to
+33 %, mean TTFT -38 %, prefill +40 %, aggregate +42 to +44 %; a reply's longest wait grows from ~0.2 s to ~0.9 s,
once per pair.

engine.py: afd_parallel_check reads both settings before the wire opens; the --parallel start line names them when
set. THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md ("GLM sliced prompt fills") records the change to the ported _fill_turn and
_part_iteration.

Tests: tests/test_glm_fill_pairs.py (CPU): the layered fake model at world 1 over the checked slice wire with the fake
pairs: prompts of 60 to 200 tokens arriving mid-decode, pairs between decode rounds and partner-less chunks sliced,
the replies the solo ones and equal to the switch off with the same cached counts and kept states; the staggered six
requests at 2 and 4 streams; grouped chunks with FP8 rings; a client leaving after a pair; K = 1, 2, 3 rounds between
two slices, K rounds after each pair, and two ranks with K = 2 (both ranks equal after every message); the settings,
their refusals of bad values, what they do where they cannot act (nothing), /health and the start line's words.

Co-authored-by: MiaAI-Lab <MiaAI-Lab@users.noreply.github.com>
Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (planning and execution), via Hermes

